    "overview": "Read about properties of matter and how matter changes, then answer the questions.",
    "passage": "Properties of Matter\n\nAll matter is made of particles too small to see. Even air is matter. When you pump up a ball, the air you push inside takes up space, and the ball gets a little heavier.\n\nScientists tell materials apart by their properties. Some properties you can see, like color and shine. Others you have to test. Is it hard or soft? Does a magnet pull on it? Does heat or electricity move through it easily? That property is called conductivity. Does it dissolve in water? That property is called solubility.\n\nMatter can change. In a physical change, like melting ice or dissolving salt, the material is still the same substance. You could get the salt back by letting the water evaporate. In a chemical change, like rust forming on a bike chain or vinegar fizzing with baking soda, new substances form. Clues to a chemical change include a new color, a gas, or heat or light given off.\n\nOne rule is always true: when matter changes, the total mass stays the same. If you mix 100 grams of water with 10 grams of salt, the salt water has a mass of 110 grams, even though the salt seems to disappear.",
